{"id": "z2133", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 15, "onset_chunk": 14, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "The correct answer is D Streetcar and trolley lines extending from the CBD Star-shaped city patterns were often created by streetcar and tro lly lines that radiated out from a central business district These transportation systems connected the center of town to surrounding areas, promoting development along their routes and forming the distinctive star shape.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 21, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z2141", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 29, "onset_chunk": 28, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so the patient has multiple sc lerosis and a sudden loss of vision in one eye with pal lar optic disc on funduscopic exam. This is classic for optic neur itis. Optic neu ritis is an inflammation of the optic nerve, which is often due to demyelination. So let's look at the options: A media l long itudinal fascic ulus. That' s part of the spinal cord that' s involved in coordinating movements. Not directly related to vision or the opt ic nerve. B, o culomotor nerve control s eye movement and pupil size but not vision. C, opt ic nerve carries visual information from the eye to the brain. Damage here causes vision loss, especially if it's de myelinated as in MS. D, tri geminal nerve controls facial sensation and motor functions, not vision. So the correct answer is C, opt ic nerve. The demyelination is happening in the optic nerve causing the inflammation and pallor seen on exam.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 47, "carrier_ended": null}
