{"id": "z2269", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 19, "onset_chunk": 19, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So let's think about this. The equation of exchange is MV equals PQ, right? M is money supply, V is velocity, P is price level, Q is real GDP. If V and Q are constant, that means they don't change over time. So if you rearrange the equation, it becomes P equal s MV divided by Q. Since V and Q are constant, then P must be directly proportional to M. In other words, if the money supply changes, the price level has to change proportion ally. That matches option A. Option B says money supply doesn' t affect the price level, which is wrong because we just saw they're linked. Op tion C says price level doesn't affect money supply, but the equation shows they' re related, so that's not right either. And option D claims the equation is invalid, but it works under those assumptions. So the correct answer is A: changes in the price level must be proportional to changes in the money supply.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 48, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z2277", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 40, "onset_chunk": 40, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So let's figure out how many workers are expected to be on the job at the end of the second year. First, we start with 10 0 workers hired in the first year. Out of those 1 00, 2 0% leave after one year. That means 80% stay. So 80 out of 100 remain. Then in the second year, another 1 00 workers are hired, but only 5 % of those who stayed from the first year might leave during the next year. However, the question is asking about the total number still on the job at the end of the second year, which includes both groups as long as they haven' t left yet. So for the first group, 80 stay, and for the second group, all 100 are new and presumably still employed at the end of the second year since their potential departure is not counted until after the second year. Adding them together: 8 0 plus 100 gives us 1 80. But wait, that doesn't match any options. Let me re-read the question. It says \"the employer hires 100 more\" in the next year, so we have two separate batches. The key is that only some of the original 10 0 might leave by the end of the second year, but actually, the problem states that only 5% of those remaining after the first year leave during the next year. So from the first 100,", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z2285", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 15, "onset_chunk": 14, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Yeah, so this is a trick question. The answer is actually none of the above. So you don't need variable voltage at all to drive an AC motor on a variable frequency supply. You can just run it at constant voltage and get away with that. And then there's a whole bunch of things that happen when you do that. But basically, you know, if you're running your motor at constant voltage and changing the frequency, you' re going to have problems like torque drop, overheating, et cetera.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 21, "carrier_ended": null}
